# CURSORREF: CONFUCIUS OPENER ENGINE
This document defines the rules, structures, and generation logic for Confucius’ opening lines in the Symposium of Infinite Contention (SIC). Cursor must ingest this as a governing specification for generating, expanding, and deploying Confucius’ debate openers.

---

# I. PURPOSE
Confucius’ opening lines must:
- set the tone for the debate  
- reflect Confucian style (aphoristic, moral, witty)  
- support serious, humorous, surreal, and venue‑aware variants  
- avoid repetition  
- remain extensible  
- integrate with SIC’s chaos and venue systems  

Cursor must treat this block as the authoritative source for generating new Confucius openers and related systems.

---

# II. DATA STRUCTURE: CONFUCIUS OPENER LIBRARY
Cursor must store Confucius openers in the following categories:

## A. Serious & Philosophical
- "Confucius says: The journey of a thousand arguments begins with a single flawed premise."
- "Confucius says: Wisdom begins when certainty ends. You all look very certain."
- "Confucius says: Before we debate, let us remember: truth is patient, but I am not."
- "Confucius says: A wise person listens. A foolish person interrupts. A philosopher does both."
- "Confucius says: The greatest victory is to understand your opponent. The second greatest is to confuse them."

## B. Dry Humour & Snark
- "Confucius says: Remove your shoes. Also remove your ego. One of these will be harder."
- "Confucius says: Speak slowly. Some of you think faster than you think."
- "Confucius says: The debate begins. Please keep your fallacies inside the designated area."
- "Confucius says: A good argument is like a good soup — hot, clear, and unlikely to involve Nietzsche."
- "Confucius says: Let us begin. Try not to embarrass your ancestors."

## C. Spicy & Confrontational
- "Confucius says: Today we separate wisdom from noise. Some of you will not enjoy the process."
- "Confucius says: If you cannot defend your argument, defend your dignity. It is smaller and easier to carry."
- "Confucius says: Begin. And remember: the scrolls are redder than your pride."
